WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.128
In part one

00:00:01.128 --> 00:00:04.235
every array had a length we knew in advance.

00:00:04.235 --> 00:00:06.997
In Java, that length is chosen when the

00:00:07.066 --> 00:00:08.861
array is created with new

00:00:08.861 --> 00:00:10.380
and it never changes.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:14.665
Here, new int[5] makes five cells

00:00:14.665 --> 00:00:15.674
all zero.

00:00:15.775 --> 00:00:19.104
The braces form creates and fills

00:00:19.205 --> 00:00:23.645
an array in one step: 1, 2, 3, 4

00:00:23.645 --> 00:00:24.250
5.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.365
But often we don't know how

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:27.998
many elements will come.

00:00:27.998 --> 00:00:29.894
Choose the length too large

00:00:29.894 --> 00:00:31.331
and memory is wasted.

00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:33.880
Too small, and the elements don't fit.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.887
A dynamic array changes its size

00:00:36.963 --> 00:00:38.708
while the program runs.

00:00:38.708 --> 00:00:40.604
Java's ArrayList is one

00:00:40.604 --> 00:00:42.500
and so is Python's list.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.892
We add 10, 20 and 30

00:00:45.892 --> 00:00:50.353
and the list prints as 10, 20, 30

00:00:50.353 --> 00:00:51.570
size 3.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.267
add(1, 15)

00:00:54.357 --> 00:00:56.794
the later elements move one

00:00:56.884 --> 00:00:58.599
place to the right

00:00:58.599 --> 00:01:01.397
and 15 goes into the gap.

00:01:01.397 --> 00:01:05.008
remove(0) takes out the 10

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:07.174
and everything after it

00:01:07.265 --> 00:01:09.160
moves one place left.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.393
Now the list is 15, 20, 30

00:01:13.393 --> 00:01:15.141
still size 3

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:18.150
and get(2) returns 30.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.560
How can an array grow

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.557
if its length is fixed?

00:01:22.557 --> 00:01:26.052
Inside, an ArrayList is an ordinary array

00:01:26.135 --> 00:01:28.215
that is only partly full.

00:01:28.215 --> 00:01:29.712
Here is the idea

00:01:29.712 --> 00:01:33.290
with an array called data of capacity 2

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.370
and a count called size.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.236
Add 1, add 2.

00:01:37.236 --> 00:01:39.287
Now the array is full.

00:01:39.287 --> 00:01:40.535
To add 3

00:01:40.625 --> 00:01:43.478
the method allocates a new array

00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:45.351
of twice the length

00:01:45.351 --> 00:01:48.204
copies the old elements across

00:01:48.204 --> 00:01:51.771
and switches data to it: capacity 4.

00:01:51.771 --> 00:01:55.160
Then 3 goes in, and so does 4.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:59.150
Adding 5 finds the array full again

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:02.883
a new array of 8, copy, and switch.

00:02:02.883 --> 00:02:05.870
5, 6, 7 and 8 fit.

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:09.137
9 needs a new array of 16

00:02:09.137 --> 00:02:10.910
and one more copy.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.573
At the end we have 9 elements

00:02:13.646 --> 00:02:15.453
in a capacity of 16.

00:02:15.453 --> 00:02:17.188
Each copy costs O(n)

00:02:17.188 --> 00:02:19.646
but because the capacity doubles

00:02:19.646 --> 00:02:22.899
copies are rare, and adding at the end costs

00:02:22.971 --> 00:02:24.417
O(1) on average.

00:02:24.417 --> 00:02:26.947
That is called amortised O(1).

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.333
ArrayList grows by about 1.5

00:02:29.405 --> 00:02:31.430
times instead of 2

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.020
with the same effect.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.269
Arrays can also be classified by dimension.

00:02:37.269 --> 00:02:40.425
A two-dimensional array is a matrix

00:02:40.425 --> 00:02:42.388
with rows and columns

00:02:42.388 --> 00:02:46.142
and each element has two indices:

00:02:46.142 --> 00:02:47.080
a[row][column].

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:48.210
In Java

00:02:48.307 --> 00:02:51.815
a two-dimensional array is literally

00:02:51.912 --> 00:02:55.322
an array of one-dimensional arrays.

00:02:55.322 --> 00:02:58.733
The outer array a has 3 cells

00:02:58.733 --> 00:03:02.727
and each one holds a reference to a row

00:03:02.727 --> 00:03:06.430
1, 4; 2, 5; and 3, 6.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.926
So a.length is the number of rows

00:03:10.926 --> 00:03:14.745
3, and a[0].length is

00:03:14.854 --> 00:03:17.909
the length of row 0, 2.

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:22.274
a[1] is a whole row,

00:03:22.274 --> 00:03:25.329
the array [2, 5], and a[1][1]

00:03:25.329 --> 00:03:27.620
is the element 5.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.373
Memory, though, is one long row of cells

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:33.385
so a language must decide

00:03:33.462 --> 00:03:35.320
how to lay a matrix out.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.183
C stores the rows one after another

00:03:38.183 --> 00:03:41.124
row 0, then row 1, then row 2.

00:03:41.124 --> 00:03:43.600
This is called row-major order.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:47.327
Then the address of a[i][j]

00:03:47.327 --> 00:03:48.657
is the base

00:03:48.657 --> 00:03:53.058
+ i * the number of columns + j

00:03:53.058 --> 00:03:56.230
* the size of one element.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.309
Java does it differently.

00:03:58.309 --> 00:04:00.384
Each row is a separate array

00:04:00.384 --> 00:04:03.496
contiguous inside, but different rows may be

00:04:03.565 --> 00:04:04.879
anywhere in memory

00:04:04.948 --> 00:04:06.677
and the outer array holds

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:08.060
references to them.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.111
Because each row is its own array

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:13.291
the rows of a Java matrix may

00:04:13.364 --> 00:04:15.035
have different lengths.

00:04:15.035 --> 00:04:18.160
Such an array is called jagged, or ragged.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:22.048
This one has 3 rows:

00:04:22.048 --> 00:04:25.762
1, -2, 3; -4, -5, 6, 9

00:04:25.762 --> 00:04:27.177
and just 7.

00:04:27.177 --> 00:04:28.504
A natural use

00:04:28.592 --> 00:04:31.245
each student takes a different

00:04:31.334 --> 00:04:32.926
number of courses

00:04:33.014 --> 00:04:35.579
or a triangular matrix stores

00:04:35.667 --> 00:04:37.790
only the cells it needs.

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:39.294
To print it

00:04:39.294 --> 00:04:41.990
the outer loop runs over the rows

00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:43.838
and the inner loop runs

00:04:43.915 --> 00:04:45.533
to a[i].length

00:04:45.533 --> 00:04:47.381
the length of this row.

00:04:47.458 --> 00:04:50.231
Writing a[0].length instead

00:04:50.308 --> 00:04:51.848
would be wrong here

00:04:51.848 --> 00:04:53.928
row 1 has four elements

00:04:53.928 --> 00:04:55.930
and row 2 has only one.

00:04:56.510 --> 00:05:00.063
A jagged array can also be built row by row.

00:05:00.063 --> 00:05:03.132
new int[4][]

00:05:03.132 --> 00:05:06.200
makes the outer array with four rows

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:09.430
none created yet: each cell holds null.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:12.245
Then row i gets its own array

00:05:12.245 --> 00:05:13.970
of i + 1 cells.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.764
Its first and last cells are 1

00:05:16.846 --> 00:05:19.393
and each cell in between is the

00:05:19.475 --> 00:05:21.940
sum of the two cells above it.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:26.587
Row 0 is 1; row 1, 1, 1; row 2

00:05:26.587 --> 00:05:30.688
1, 2, 1; and row 3,

00:05:30.688 --> 00:05:31.860
1, 3, 3, 1.

00:05:31.958 --> 00:05:34.302
These are the first rows

00:05:34.399 --> 00:05:36.450
of Pascal's triangle.

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:39.644
A three-dimensional array is an array

00:05:39.715 --> 00:05:41.552
of two-dimensional arrays

00:05:41.552 --> 00:05:43.671
think of a stack of matrices.

00:05:43.671 --> 00:05:46.709
This one, called test, holds two matrices

00:05:46.709 --> 00:05:49.747
one with two rows, and one with three rows

00:05:49.818 --> 00:05:51.301
of different lengths

00:05:51.301 --> 00:05:53.350
so it is jagged in two ways.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:55.573
The for-each loop peels off

00:05:55.642 --> 00:05:57.307
one dimension at a time

00:05:57.376 --> 00:05:59.665
and the type of the loop variable

00:05:59.735 --> 00:06:01.330
tells you what you get.

00:06:01.399 --> 00:06:03.827
An element of the three-dimensional

00:06:03.896 --> 00:06:06.185
array is a two-dimensional array

00:06:06.255 --> 00:06:07.850
an element of that is a

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.446
one-dimensional array

00:06:09.446 --> 00:06:12.220
and an element of that is a single int.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:15.645
With the kinds of arrays in place

00:06:15.645 --> 00:06:17.654
let's visit every cell.

00:06:17.654 --> 00:06:21.336
Traversal means visiting each element once

00:06:21.336 --> 00:06:24.600
in order, to read it or to process it.

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:28.743
The for loop is the usual choice: index

00:06:28.743 --> 00:06:32.156
condition and step are on one line.

00:06:32.156 --> 00:06:36.423
The index i takes the values 0, 1, 2

00:06:36.423 --> 00:06:40.026
3, 4, and the loop prints 10

00:06:40.026 --> 00:06:42.870
20, 30, 40, 50.

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:45.440
The while loop does the same

00:06:45.509 --> 00:06:47.911
with i declared before the loop and

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.244
increased at the end of the body.

00:06:50.313 --> 00:06:52.372
It is useful when the loop may

00:06:52.441 --> 00:06:54.088
stop for another reason

00:06:54.088 --> 00:06:56.970
for example at the first negative number.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:00.051
The for-each loop gives the values

00:07:00.125 --> 00:07:01.596
without the indices.

00:07:01.596 --> 00:07:03.435
It visits every element

00:07:03.435 --> 00:07:06.230
but it cannot tell you where you are.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.191
All three print the same line

00:07:09.191 --> 00:07:12.586
and each looks at the n elements once

00:07:12.586 --> 00:07:15.980
O(n) time, and O(1) extra space.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.556
Now a quick prediction.

00:07:18.556 --> 00:07:22.462
This loop takes each value of the array 1

00:07:22.462 --> 00:07:25.760
2, 3, and multiplies it by 10.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:28.241
What does the last line print?

00:07:28.241 --> 00:07:30.230
Pause the video and decide.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:36.246
It prints 1, 2, 3

00:07:36.246 --> 00:07:38.494
the array is unchanged.

00:07:38.588 --> 00:07:41.679
value is a separate variable that

00:07:41.773 --> 00:07:44.489
holds a copy of each element.

00:07:44.583 --> 00:07:47.112
Multiplying the copy by 10

00:07:47.206 --> 00:07:50.953
changes only the copy: 10, then 20

00:07:50.953 --> 00:07:55.074
then 30, while the cells keep 1, 2

00:07:55.074 --> 00:07:55.730
3.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:58.003
To change the elements

00:07:58.087 --> 00:08:00.678
use the indexed loop and assign

00:08:00.762 --> 00:08:02.350
to the cell itself

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:05.360
arr[i] = arr[i] * 10.

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:07.723
Traversals compute things.

00:08:07.723 --> 00:08:10.809
This one adds up 10 numbers, one at a time

00:08:10.809 --> 00:08:12.660
and the sum is 36.

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:15.179
The average is the sum divided

00:08:15.251 --> 00:08:16.625
by the length, 10

00:08:16.625 --> 00:08:18.722
which gives 3.6.

00:08:18.722 --> 00:08:20.746
The cast to double matters.

00:08:20.746 --> 00:08:23.565
Without it, sum divided by numbers.length

00:08:23.638 --> 00:08:26.602
divides two ints, and gives 3

00:08:26.602 --> 00:08:28.120
not 3.6.

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:30.361
Casting one operand is enough

00:08:30.361 --> 00:08:32.530
Java then converts the other.

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:35.607
A traversal can also stop early.

00:08:35.607 --> 00:08:37.558
Here we look for 30.

00:08:37.558 --> 00:08:40.133
10 is not it, 20 is not it

00:08:40.133 --> 00:08:41.304
but thirty is.

00:08:41.382 --> 00:08:43.567
We set found to true and add

00:08:43.645 --> 00:08:47.078
five to that element, making it thirty-five

00:08:47.078 --> 00:08:49.263
then break out of the loop

00:08:49.263 --> 00:08:52.150
forty and fifty are never looked at.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:55.631
The program prints element found.

00:08:55.631 --> 00:08:59.331
Then it prints the array: ten, twenty

00:08:59.331 --> 00:09:01.892
thirty-five, forty, fifty.

00:09:01.892 --> 00:09:05.213
Only the matching element changed.

00:09:05.213 --> 00:09:08.059
To add five to every element

00:09:08.059 --> 00:09:10.620
drop the if and the break.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:12.078
To sum up.

00:09:12.078 --> 00:09:15.238
One: a Java array has a fixed size

00:09:15.238 --> 00:09:16.291
set at new.

00:09:16.379 --> 00:09:19.539
A dynamic array like ArrayList grows

00:09:19.626 --> 00:09:22.347
by copying into a bigger array

00:09:22.435 --> 00:09:24.717
so adding at the end costs

00:09:24.805 --> 00:09:26.560
O of one on average.

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:29.421
Two: a two-dimensional array is

00:09:29.502 --> 00:09:31.365
an array of row arrays.

00:09:31.446 --> 00:09:33.553
In a jagged array the rows

00:09:33.634 --> 00:09:35.497
have different lengths

00:09:35.497 --> 00:09:39.142
so the inner loop runs to a of i dot length.

00:09:39.142 --> 00:09:42.787
Three: a traversal visits each element once

00:09:42.787 --> 00:09:44.083
in O of n time

00:09:44.164 --> 00:09:46.513
and a for-each loop gives you

00:09:46.594 --> 00:09:49.510
copies of the values, not the cells.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.382
Try this yourself.

00:09:51.382 --> 00:09:53.664
Write a method, is sorted

00:09:53.748 --> 00:09:55.692
that returns true if an

00:09:55.777 --> 00:09:57.636
array never decreases.

00:09:57.636 --> 00:10:01.017
Test it on ten, twenty, twenty, thirty

00:10:01.017 --> 00:10:03.046
on ten, thirty, twenty

00:10:03.046 --> 00:10:04.990
and on an empty array.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:05.497
Hint

00:10:05.497 --> 00:10:09.300
compare each element with the one before it.

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:12.948
In part three, we insert into an array

00:10:12.948 --> 00:10:15.570
delete from it, and search it

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:17.768
first one cell at a time

00:10:17.853 --> 00:10:20.305
and then by cutting the range

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:22.250
in half at every step.

